{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 880 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "f9e6370ec80898fc64a9b7daa347ba86d2b9ccf200bc8c197945b6fd31a348cc" :edb/db-before-t 879 :edb/db-after-t 880 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534192 50 #inst "2026-10-11T17:36:18.976Z" 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186166506 1074 "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n![](images/17/17657d7db880fc0ababc24511039a9d7446952f7521e31ae3351adbb6b28ee61.png)\nthe formula for the general solution is\n![](images/44/44a286f1a4c15598038f38cdb4140534bac24caacb36f32ee4ef9cbe05b3e5e1.png)\n\nFirst, we write our differential equation in matrix form:\n\n![](images/d9/d9b9ac7fc20d076110f99476fc48d3c00c4206dc37fb2a929893fcbbb77d6526.png)\n\nSince the matrix of the differential system is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n![](images/4f/4f7cfa979200fccf811a7e0d9a633b7919b33b7740c635a921b75ad09995e700.png)" 13194139534192 17592186045417 false] [17592186166506 1074 "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n![](images/63/630f2ae400ab4ccd212138703f93516384c23b284f5ecac5b8c459964f436d0b.svg)\nthe formula for the general solution is\n![](images/ff/fffb7d52ff1ec576d336b3498e40ea433546d971a398550848359cda657f957c.svg)\n\nFirst, we write our differential equation in matrix form:\n\n![](images/d5/d5c965f4aa3a43689e92d60295049b1d7388c0d7eaf8b8ee93c1bd7eec4af207.svg)\n\nSince the matrix of the differential system is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n![](images/e4/e4b23c56134ac2b5f813ebbe4aa89f4ca4fc35a9c96be1471b4d37915270e46f.svg)" 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186166507 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n![](images/11/11d3e9693d6aa5f2b7836f4efad788fc8693d8e28ed2f0d8b5bf3ea2b9937850.svg)" 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186166507 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n![](images/5c/5cc29f7492fded69b4100e10329c831cca0734a29966cd1aac2e8ad017636719.png)" 13194139534192 17592186045417 false] [17592186166896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413605 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186166896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413606 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186167009 1050 420.0 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186167314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165093 13194139534192 17592186045417 false] [17592186167314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413603 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186167314 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186167314 1071 "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=\\sqrt{3}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}$" 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186167314 1071 "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n${x_{1}^{′}(t) = \\sqrt{3}x_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = \\sqrt{2}x_{2}(t)$." 13194139534192 17592186045417 false] [17592186167314 1074 "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write our differential equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of the differential system is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$" 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186167979 1064 "A **decoupled system** of ODEs is a system in which each equation involves only its own dependent variable. For a linear homogeneous system, this means each equation has the form\n\n$$\nx'_i(t) = \\lambda_i x_i(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_i$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently.  \n\nFor example, the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis decoupled, while the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis *not* decoupled.\n\nIn matrix form, a linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nis decoupled exactly when $A$ is a diagonal matrix. For example:\n\n- The system\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis decoupled since its corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\n- The system\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis *not* decoupled because its matrix is not diagonal." 13194139534192 17592186045417 false] [17592186167979 1064 "A **decoupled system** of ODEs is a system in which each equation involves only its own dependent variable. For a linear homogeneous system, this means each equation has the form\n\n$$\nx'_i(t) = \\lambda_i x_i(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_i$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently.  \n\nFor example, the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis decoupled, while the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis *not* decoupled.\n\nIn matrix form, a linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nis decoupled exactly when $A$ is a diagonal matrix. For example:\n\n- The system\n![](images/d8/d8f25a6f8f1d636e750d597907bc686acb5768f37fbbc60b0e07ee31519e3dea.svg)\nis decoupled since its corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\n- The system\n![](images/17/170c78d470ac7d7af444604f8be7eb850248a36bd2b1a1179e9c4a1c76ab19d4.svg)\nis *not* decoupled because its matrix is not diagonal." 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186167980 1064 "Now, let's consider the following decoupled system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)=-5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is diagonal, we can solve the two differential equations separately using elementary calculus:\n\n- The solution to $x_1'(t)={3}x_1(t)$ is $x_1(t)=c_1e^{{3}t}$ for $c_1\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- The solution to $x_2'(t)={-5}x_2(t)$ is $x_2(t)=c_2e^{{-5}t}$ for $c_2\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the solution of the system as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIn general, for a decoupled system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)={\\lambda }_{1}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)={\\lambda }_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe formula for the **general solution** is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNote that the set \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nalways forms a linearly independent set of fundamental solutions to the system." 13194139534192 17592186045417 false] [17592186167980 1064 "Now, let's consider the following decoupled system:\n![](images/30/3059139f7e3fc43137dcf6422cb3e2e73649e643968e980ab3fa5c2a60062172.svg)\n\nSince $A$ is diagonal, we can solve the two differential equations separately using elementary calculus:\n\n- The solution to $x_1'(t)={3}x_1(t)$ is $x_1(t)=c_1e^{{3}t}$ for $c_1\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- The solution to $x_2'(t)={-5}x_2(t)$ is $x_2(t)=c_2e^{{-5}t}$ for $c_2\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the solution of the system as\n![](images/03/03e6bf38a720a04bbfb14b7eb712693bce86838d2981a0df6fe66fefef5c5952.svg)\n\nIn general, for a decoupled system\n![](images/b9/b95e096702bebeaaae7e6b2ef9762960a4dbe8ffb5b9a46e05cdd40e1a42d484.svg)\nthe formula for the **general solution** is\n![](images/a3/a3b7aad0b63afbe8e087cb488bc0ba29a7cfaab5c5ca3f4f0bd8fe57fe15121a.svg)\n\nNote that the set \n![](images/ef/efcb42f86c9c1b64f7db619d82c8eebad19fd5f3e05a946a3d34963c4de9f334.svg)\nalways forms a linearly independent set of fundamental solutions to the system." 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186167981 1064 "Consider the following decoupled system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the *general solution* to this is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBut sometimes we may need to find a **particular solution** where we give specific values to the constants $c_1$ and $c_2.$\n\nFor example, suppose that $x_1$ and $x_2$ represent the number of birds of species $A$ and $B,$ respectively, that inhabit a particular island, and the above system models the growth rates of the two species. \n\nGiven this information, we might want to know how many birds of each type there will be after $t=3$ years. To answer this question, we need an **initial condition**, which is some extra information about the system at a particular moment in time. \n\nSuppose that at $t=0,$ there were initially $20$ birds of species $A$ and $2\\,000$ birds of species $B.$ We can write this initial condition as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(0) \\\\ {x}_{2}(0)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, by substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we can find specific values of $c_1$ and $c_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 2\\,000\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 2\\,000\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 2\\,000\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $c_1 = 20$ and $c_2 = 2\\,000$.\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find how many birds of each type there will be after $t=3$ years by substituting $t=3$ into the particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(3) & =20[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3}+2\\,000[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20{e}^{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\,000{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20{e}^{3} \\\\ 2\\,000{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\approx [\\begin{aligned}401.7 \\\\ 99.6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that there will be approximately $402$ birds of species $A$ and $100$ birds of species $B$ after $t=3$ years." 13194139534192 17592186045417 false] [17592186167981 1064 "Consider the following decoupled system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the *general solution* to this is given by\n\n![](images/79/79d3209f0112e97df3ece546181add70427484455248e907993fa9a617b60bd0.svg)\n\nBut sometimes we may need to find a **particular solution** where we give specific values to the constants $c_1$ and $c_2.$\n\nFor example, suppose that $x_1$ and $x_2$ represent the number of birds of species $A$ and $B,$ respectively, that inhabit a particular island, and the above system models the growth rates of the two species. \n\nGiven this information, we might want to know how many birds of each type there will be after $t=3$ years. To answer this question, we need an **initial condition**, which is some extra information about the system at a particular moment in time. \n\nSuppose that at $t=0,$ there were initially $20$ birds of species $A$ and $2\\,000$ birds of species $B.$ We can write this initial condition as follows:\n\n![](images/6e/6e83003ab2d4d19328189f5cff0d3542a48af224e17cf2d5de0d6cddc5971548.svg)\n\nThen, by substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we can find specific values of $c_1$ and $c_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 2\\,000\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 2\\,000\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 2\\,000\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $c_1 = 20$ and $c_2 = 2\\,000$.\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is given by \n\n![](images/f1/f101144c98365f20dce4ce0fb6e41cc2f8cf55b64cc19a77fa63da6a4a48415e.svg)\n\nFinally, we can find how many birds of each type there will be after $t=3$ years by substituting $t=3$ into the particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(3) & =20[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3}+2\\,000[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20{e}^{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\,000{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20{e}^{3} \\\\ 2\\,000{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}401.7 \\\\ 99.6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that there will be approximately $402$ birds of species $A$ and $100$ birds of species $B$ after $t=3$ years." 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186413587 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{2}t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186413587 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186413587 1083 #uuid "0abeb303-65a1-580a-8d0c-2e6a1c960b4d" 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186413588 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186413588 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186413588 1083