true] [17592186413605 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413604 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186413605 1069 "q-331097" 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186413605 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186413605 1071 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-7{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=9{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186413605 1072 #uuid "96f65a49-2a61-5981-a404-aa212d4de13c" 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186413605 1074 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{2}(t)$ and ${x}_{1}(t).$\n\n- System III is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-7{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=9{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186413606 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413602 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186413606 1069 "q-331980" 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186413606 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186413606 1071 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t)$" 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186413606 1072 #uuid "ac9ae842-1c73-58e9-9ed3-756e4fa56ec1" 13194139534192 17592186045417 true] [17592186413606 1074 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently.  \n\nIn matrix form, a linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ is decoupled if $A$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- Systems I and III are decoupled since their corresponding matrices are diagonal.\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because its matrix is not diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139534192 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0abeb303-65a1-580a-8d0c-2e6a1c960b4d" 17592186413587 "answer-18398e71-d597-5623-af51-1629bf876027" 17592186413588 "answer-4677b7a7-7f61-5a88-a5ee-a34ee62c287d" 17592186413589 "answer-59ef9340-52d9-5cb4-999f-d1140490bfc4" 17592186413590 "answer-5d9de9ca-9cf2-557a-8cdb-19ad0d5e57b5" 17592186413591 "answer-69db9e77-56bd-5ad8-9ac5-e356be91510d" 17592186413592 "answer-7ad62917-fe79-54d5-9e2a-eb52a863023e" 17592186413593 "answer-91bf1bed-82dd-5957-bd20-b0c4787407e0" 17592186413594 "answer-96da30d2-eb7e-540b-b235-30c2de13031a" 17592186413595 "answer-a5551b66-4909-5792-8d3d-3a30ec491dc1" 17592186413596 "answer-ad2050e5-3099-5de8-9248-cf99c56b8941" 17592186413597 "answer-b09a08e9-f214-5a5c-b110-f1010c4b03ee" 17592186413598 "answer-c068b9da-8858-56ed-90bd-f563b1b66009" 17592186413599 "answer-c90c80ee-ce2e-5334-9b0a-1654558fcf8c" 17592186413600 "answer-f322c546-6f3c-57e2-a